* feat(terminal): kubernetes backend — pod per session, existing-claim workspace [daveey] (#242)

* feat(terminal): kubernetes backend — pod per session, PVC per profile [daveey]

New built-in terminal backend `kubernetes` (tools/environments/kubernetes.py):

- One pod per (profile, session), named hermes-<profile>-<sha>, labelled
  app.kubernetes.io/managed-by=hermes + hermes/profile; reused while running,
  recreated when missing/failed; bounded Ready wait that fails fast on
  image-pull / container-config errors (EnvironmentConnectionError).
- One ReadWriteOnce PVC per profile (hermes-ws-<profile>) at /workspace,
  created when missing, never deleted by the backend.
- Pod hardening: runAsNonRoot uid 1000, no privilege escalation, drop ALL
  caps, RuntimeDefault seccomp, no service-account token, requests=limits.
- Commands run through the exec websocket (kubernetes.stream) under the base
  snapshot/cwd wrapping; exit code from the status channel; stdin is
  length-framed; a timeout kills the remote process tree via a pidfile.
- Idle past terminal.kubernetes.idle_timeout or cleanup() deletes the pod (PVC
  kept); hermes/last-used annotation stamped for an external reaper.
- Namespace/image/resources come only from the profile's terminal.kubernetes
  config. Auth: in-cluster, else kubeconfig (optional context).
- kubernetes==36.0.0 as the optional `kubernetes` extra (37.x needs a newer
  certifi than core pins); registered in the builder/requirement tables,
  backend sets, doctor, setup wizard, dashboard probe/picker, docs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* feat(terminal): kubernetes backend needs no PVC rights; uid, kubeconfig, scheduling knobs [daveey]

The first consumer's ServiceAccount can only manage pods (+ pods/exec, pods/log)
in its namespace, so the backend no longer creates a per-profile PVC:

- terminal.kubernetes.workspace_pvc / workspace_subpath mount an EXISTING claim
  (e.g. RWX/EFS) at /workspace; unset = emptyDir. No PVC API is ever called
  (pvc_size / storage_class removed).
- run_as_user / run_as_group / fs_group (default 1000, uid 0 refused) so the
  pod uid matches existing workspace ownership.
- kubeconfig: explicit path wins; else in-cluster, else KUBECONFIG/default.
- node_selector / tolerations passthrough; ephemeral_storage request=limit.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* feat(terminal): kubernetes backend — configurable workspace_mount; pod HOME on its own emptyDir [daveey]

A shared workspace claim mounted at workspace_mount (default /workspace) with
its subPath; HOME and the ~/.hermes sync move to an emptyDir at /home/hermes
so skills, cache and credential files never land on the shared claim.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* fix(terminal): kubernetes backend sends its token with kubernetes 36.0.0

36.0.0's kubeconfig and in-cluster loaders store the token under api_key['authorization'], but
Configuration.auth_settings() reads only api_key['BearerToken'], so every request went without
Authorization and EKS answered 401 (first live pool sandbox, 2026-10-30). Mirror the key on
the default configuration, and after every token refresh. A no-op for clients that read
'authorization'. New test uses the real client (skips without the kubernetes extra).

